{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"c362051b9bc5d311be54026d8e63af2b85628363bef6c427375a0a0e446ee39d","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_70a81dce9aa58d9383076a7325ffc08471c608370678d4e56c16cee5229f32e1","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a technically sound and well-motivated method for combining algorithmic structure with end-to-end learning. The experimental design is appropriate for the scope, effectively demonstrating data efficiency and the feasibility of unsupervised learning. However, the evaluation is limited by the use of simple, low-dimensional simulation tasks and a lack of statistical significance testing, which constrains the generalizability of the claims.","paper_id":"ByvJuTigl","paper_identity_sha256":"224e2370322a9d92c1d2396d1eb584dede056247391034a503e7ce5d152bdf1e","prompt_identity_sha256":"28ab42e61f13ae5da5bb19494ec2d994a8f350f325f7ab5f42834ad86bd0552e","raw_response_sha256":"b3df3a6034b36878fcc11f5fe2d02b7f1d51dd5d8b0c5818cb7c160133d11142","request_identity_sha256":"6c18bc46346dd2a118973b1926d83840911de453b22db28fc065edf66419cb77","reviewer_identity_sha256":"c1cd7c7fdd4a2cbeb45c2f21af982ad0f30bffae8ae148e3eba36479685cf360","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"312e1bf2eaae4236232941ece8b032cb4a580ba63c99589dd8e4e654194f1754","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-012","SRC-013","SRC-016","SRC-017","SRC-019","SRC-021","SRC-022","SRC-023"],"review_text":"The proposed End-to-End Learnable Histogram Filter (E2E-HF) is mathematically consistent and correctly implements the differentiable structure of a Bayes filter. The authors provide clear formulations for the prediction and measurement update steps, ensuring that the motion and measurement models are differentiable to allow for backpropagation through time. The use of a convolutional approach for the motion model and element-wise multiplication for the measurement update is a sound technical choice that balances computational efficiency with the preservation of the filter's probabilistic structure. The derivation of the unsupervised learning objective, which relies on predicting future observations, is logically grounded in the filter's architecture and provides a valid mechanism for learning without state labels.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-MET"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-024","SRC-025","SRC-026","SRC-027","SRC-028","SRC-029","SRC-030","SRC-031"],"review_text":"The experimental setup is adequate for a proof-of-concept study, utilizing two distinct localization tasks (1D hallway and 2D drone) to evaluate data efficiency, end-to-end optimization, and unsupervised learning. The choice of baselines, including standard Histogram Filters (HF) and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks, is appropriate for isolating the benefits of the algorithmic prior and the end-to-end training paradigm. The evaluation metrics (MSE, accuracy, and observation prediction) are well-suited to the state estimation problem. However, the experiments are limited to simple, low-dimensional simulation environments with binary observations, which may not fully capture the complexity of real-world robotic state estimation. The lack of experiments on more complex or high-dimensional tasks limits the generalizability of the findings.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-017","SRC-018","SRC-021","SRC-025","SRC-027"],"review_text":"The paper provides sufficient detail for the core method, including the architecture of the measurement model (three hidden layers of 32 ReLUs) and the specific form of the motion model used in experiments (linear motion with Gaussian noise). The discretization of the state space and the parameters for the learning objectives (e.g., subsequence length C=32) are specified. However, the paper defers many implementation details, such as learning rates, optimizer settings, and specific training protocols, to an Appendix A that is not included in the provided text. This omission hinders full reproducibility, as the exact hyperparameters and training procedures are not visible in the main body of the paper.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-REP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-008","SRC-009","SRC-024","SRC-027"],"review_text":"The paper positions its work effectively within the context of combining algorithms and machine learning, specifically differentiating itself from prior work on differentiable Kalman filters (Haarnoja et al., 2016) by using a histogram representation to handle multiple hypotheses. The comparison against standard Histogram Filters and LSTMs is relevant and directly addresses the paper's claims about data efficiency and the benefits of end-to-end learning. The related work section provides a clear taxonomy of how algorithms and learning can be combined, which helps contextualize the contribution. However, the comparison is limited to these specific baselines, and there is no comparison against other state-of-the-art deep learning-based state estimation methods that might not rely on explicit filter structures.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-CMP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-026","SRC-027","SRC-029"],"review_text":"The paper reports results with means and standard errors for the learning curves in the hallway task, which is a positive step towards statistical rigor. However, the drone task results in Table 1 are presented as single-point estimates without any indication of variance or confidence intervals. Furthermore, there are no statistical significance tests to determine whether the observed differences between methods are statistically meaningful. The reliance on single runs for the drone task and the lack of significance testing for the hallway task weaken the robustness of the empirical claims, particularly regarding the magnitude of the performance improvements.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-STA"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"58305aaaffd4e05654ec5cdc7340c59508ed09a217ff43c2dbaa7a0daf43197c","paper_id":"ByvJuTigl","paper_ordinal":83,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"b3df3a6034b36878fcc11f5fe2d02b7f1d51dd5d8b0c5818cb7c160133d11142","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"ByvJuTigl","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"6ca314386fc37b2040ed3e65a280504784578da8d68ebbf1ab9c7eed4fe3d507","request_identity_sha256":"6c18bc46346dd2a118973b1926d83840911de453b22db28fc065edf66419cb77","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v5","usage":{"completion_tokens":1254,"prompt_tokens":18228,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":19482},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"6c18bc46346dd2a118973b1926d83840911de453b22db28fc065edf66419cb77","role":"QUAL","role_ordinal":0,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}